.docname {Testing}
.include {docs}

## Depend on `KarboneClient`

Code that calls Karbone should depend on the `KarboneClient` interface (`templates`, `renders`, `status()`), not on the
concrete `Karbone` class. `Karbone` is the real, network-backed implementation; `com.ekino.oss.karbone.testing.FakeKarbone` is an
in-memory double implementing the same interface, so a service can be tested without any HTTP or Docker involved.

```kotlin
class InvoiceService(private val karbone: KarboneClient) {
  context(_: Raise<KarboneError>)
  suspend fun renderInvoice(id: TemplateId, number: String): RenderedDocument =
    karbone.renders.render(TemplateSource.id(id), RenderData.raw("""{"number":"$number"}"""))
}
```

## `FakeKarbone`

`FakeKarbone` stores templates in memory (keyed by the same SHA-256 Carbone would use), so the hash-first render flow works
exactly as with the real client.

```kotlin
val fake = FakeKarbone()

// pre-load a template so a TemplateSource.Remote id resolves
val id = fake.addTemplate(templateBytes)

val document = either { fake.renders.render(TemplateSource.id(id), RenderData.raw("{}")) }
  .getOrElse { error("render failed") }
```

Key surface:

- **`addTemplate(content, options)`** — pre-loads a template, returning its (SHA-256) `TemplateId`, so renders and downloads
  against a known id work without going through `upload`.
- **`calls`** — every call made on the fake, in order, as a sealed `Call` value (one variant per `KarboneClient` method — see
  the [API reference](@/api/index.html) for the full list). Useful to assert what a service under test actually did.
- **`rendered`** — every `RenderedDocument` produced so far, in order.
- **`failNextWith(error)`** — the *next* call, whatever it is, raises `error` instead of running. Use it to test a single
  failure path without standing up a whole failing scenario.
- **`renderer`** — a `FakeKarbone.Renderer` (a `fun interface`) producing the bytes of a rendered document from a
  `RenderRequest` (`templateId`, `template` bytes, `data`, `options`). The default renderer writes a small JSON description
  of the request; swap it for a custom one to assert on realistic-looking output, or to simulate a specific format.
- **`reset()`** — clears stored templates, pending async renders, recorded calls, rendered documents and any pending
  `failNextWith` error.
- **`status`** — a mutable `ApiStatus` returned by `status()`; defaults to a successful fake status.

### Example test

```kotlin
class InvoiceServiceSpec :
  ShouldSpec({
    should("render an invoice and record the call") {
      val fake = FakeKarbone()
      val id = fake.addTemplate("template bytes".toByteArray())
      val service = InvoiceService(fake)

      val document = either { service.renderInvoice(id, "F-1") }.shouldBeInstanceOf<Either.Right<RenderedDocument>>().value

      document.fileName shouldNotBe null
      fake.calls.filterIsInstance<FakeKarbone.Call.Render>() shouldHaveSize 1
    }

    should("propagate a template-not-found error") {
      val fake = FakeKarbone()
      fake.failNextWith(KarboneError.TemplateNotFound(TemplateId("missing"), "gone"))
      val service = InvoiceService(fake)

      val result = either { service.renderInvoice(TemplateId("missing"), "F-1") }
      result.shouldBeInstanceOf<Either.Left<KarboneError>>().value.shouldBeInstanceOf<KarboneError.TemplateNotFound>()
    }
  })
```

A plain JUnit 5 test reads the same way, just without Kotest's `ShouldSpec` DSL — `FakeKarbone` and Arrow's `either`/`Either`
are ordinary Kotlin, no test-framework dependency involved.

## Karbone's own tests

Karbone's own integration tests (`CarboneContainerSpec`, `CarboneAuthContainerSpec`) run against a real `carbone-ee` container
via [Testcontainers](https://testcontainers.com/), covering both authentication-disabled and JWT-authenticated setups. They
are skipped automatically when Docker is not available, and are a good reference for exact request/response shapes if you
need to go beyond what `FakeKarbone` simulates.

## Blocking code under test

Code written against `KarboneBlocking` is tested the same way: the facade is built over `KarboneClient`, so a fake slips in.

```kotlin
val fake = FakeKarbone()
val service = InvoiceService(fake.blocking())          // or KarboneBlocking.of(fake) from Java

service.generate(invoice)

fake.calls.filterIsInstance<FakeKarbone.Call.Render>() shouldHaveSize 1

fake.failNextWith(KarboneError.Unauthorized("expired"))
shouldThrow<KarboneException> { service.generate(invoice) }.error.shouldBeInstanceOf<KarboneError.Unauthorized>()
```
